    {
      "id": "verbosity_explain_water",
      "category": "verbosity_fingerprint",
      "check_type": "length_range",
      "passed": false,
      "errored": false,
      "error": null,
      "latency_ms": 3038.1,
      "prompt_tokens": 11,
      "completion_tokens": 704,
      "output": "At its most basic level, **water** is a chemical compound made up of two elements: **hydrogen and oxygen**. Its chemical formula is **$\\text{H}_2\\text{O}$**, meaning every single molecule of water consists of two hydrogen atoms bonded to one oxygen atom. \n\nHowever, to truly understand water, it helps to look at it through a few different lenses: its physical properties, its role on Earth, and its importance to life.\n\n---\n\n### 1. Unique Physical Properties\nWater is one of the most unique and unusual substances in the universe. \n\n* **The Three States:** Water is the only natural substance on Earth that exists abundantly in three physical states: **liquid** (water), **solid** (ice), and **gas** (steam or water vapor).\n* **The \"Universal Solvent\":** Because of the chemical structure of its molecules, water is exceptionally good at dissolving other substances (like salt, sugar, and minerals). This is why it is called the universal solvent—it carries nutrients and chemicals wherever it goes.\n* **Density Anomaly:** Most substances shrink and become denser when they freeze. Water, however, *expands* and becomes less dense when it turns to ice. This is why ice floats. If ice sank, lakes and oceans would freeze from the bottom up, destroying aquatic life.\n* **High Heat Capacity:** Water can absorb a lot of heat before its temperature changes significantly. This helps regulate the climate of the entire planet and keeps living organisms from overheating.\n\n### 2. Water on Earth\nWater is the defining feature of our home planet, often called the \"Blue Planet.\"\n\n* **Abundance:** About **71% of the Earth's surface** is covered in water, mostly by oceans and seas.\n* **Distribution:** The vast majority of Earth’s water (about 96.5%) is saltwater in the oceans. Only about **2.5% is freshwater**, and most of that is locked away in glaciers, ice caps, or buried deep underground. Less than 1% of Earth's water is easily accessible for human use in lakes, rivers, and soil.\n* **The Water Cycle:** Water is constantly moving in a continuous cycle driven by the sun. It evaporates from oceans and lakes into the air, condenses into clouds, falls back to the earth as rain or snow (precipitation), and flows back into bodies of water.\n\n### 3. The Essence of Life\nAs far as science knows, **life cannot exist without water.** \n\n* **Biological Solvent:** Within living organisms, water acts as the medium in which all the metabolic and cellular processes happen. It transports nutrients, flushes out waste, and regulates body temperature.\n* **Human Body:** The human body is made up of about **60% water**. The brain is roughly 73% water, the lungs are about 83%, and even bones are about 31% water. \n* **Ecosystems:** Water shapes habitats. Aquatic ecosystems (oceans, rivers, wetlands) are home to millions of species, while terrestrial ecosystems rely on precipitation to grow plants, which form the base of the food chain.\n\n### Summary\nWater is a simple molecule with extraordinary properties. It sculpts the Earth's landscape, drives our weather and climate, and acts as the literal fluid of life for every known organism."
